{"chunk_id": "12ea4919b3da", "text": "This section depicts the catastrophic escalation of the dice game between Yudhishthira and Shakuni in the Kuru assembly. After Vidura\u2019s earlier warning about kings who reject honest counsel, the narrative turns to the gambling match where Shakuni repeatedly wins through deceit. Driven by pride and the compulsion of play, Yudhishthira wagers progressively greater stakes: vast wealth, lands, his kingdom, loyal subjects, and then his own brothers\u2014Nakula, Sahadeva, Arjuna, and Bhima\u2014losing each to Shakuni\u2019s loaded dice. Finally, he stakes himself and loses. Yet the humiliation deepens when Shakuni urges him to wager Draupadi, the queen of the Pandavas. Yudhishthira describes her virtues and beauty before staking her, prompting outrage among the elders in the court. The passage marks a moral collapse and a pivotal turning point leading to Draupadi\u2019s humiliation and the eventual war of the Mahabharata.\nIn the rigged dice game, Yudhishthira loses his wealth, kingdom, brothers, and himself to Shakuni, and finally stakes Draupadi amid horrified reactions.\nThemes: gambling and deception, loss of honor, loyalty of brothers, moral downfall, fate and manipulation", "vector": [-0.00724416458979249, -0.024429716169834137, 0.008858462795615196, 0.007560297846794128, -0.025398295372724533, 0.017192278057336807, -0.03166715428233147, 0.006813684944063425, -0.01966753602027893, -0.03446527197957039, 0.030052857473492622, 0.009732875041663647, 0.02894975244998932, 0.018214667215943336, -0.007883157581090927, 0.015577980317175388, 0.03946959599852562, 0.0011812965385615826, 0.049774203449487686, -0.026541758328676224, -0.013923324644565582, -0.010546750389039516, 0.00011634299880824983, 0.01361391693353653, -0.01947920210659504, 0.02451043203473091, 0.016815608367323875, -0.018658600747585297, 0.008575960993766785, -0.014380709268152714, -0.009255311451852322, -0.005364179145544767, 0.023313160985708237, -0.018887292593717